P=[
("Ephialtes of Trachis",-520,"5th century BCE","Traitor","Malis, Greece","Betraying the Greeks at Thermopylae","Ephialtes, son of Eurydemus, was a local Greek who, according to Herodotus, went to Xerxes hoping for a reward and showed the Persians the Anopaea mountain path around the pass of Thermopylae in 480 BCE. This let the Persian Immortals surround Leonidas's force. After the Persians were defeated, the Greek council (the Amphictyons) put a price on his head; he fled to Thessaly and was later killed, though for an unrelated quarrel. In modern Greek his name means 'nightmare'.","Ephialtes_of_Trachis","T","Battle of Thermopylae; Persian Wars"),
("Arminius",-18,"c. 18 BCE – 21 CE","Germanic chieftain","Germania","Destroying three Roman legions at the Teutoburg Forest","Arminius, a prince of the Cherusci, was taken to Rome as a hostage, educated there, granted citizenship and served as an officer leading auxiliary troops. Trusted by the governor Varus, he secretly united German tribes and in 9 CE led Varus's three legions into a deadly ambush. He defeated later Roman campaigns, but was murdered by his own relatives around 21 CE, who feared he wanted to be king. In 19th-century Germany he became a nationalist hero, 'Hermann'.","Arminius","R","Battle of the Teutoburg Forest; Teutoburg Forest; Augustus; Roman Empire"),
("Marcus Junius Brutus",-85,"85–42 BCE","Roman senator","Rome","Assassinating Julius Caesar","Brutus had fought against Caesar in the civil war but was pardoned and promoted by him; rumours even said Caesar was his father. Believing Caesar would destroy the Republic, he joined Cassius in leading the conspiracy that stabbed Caesar on the Ides of March, 44 BCE; Shakespeare gave Caesar the famous line 'Et tu, Brute?' Defeated by Antony and Octavian at Philippi in 42 BCE, he took his own life. Dante placed him in the lowest circle of Hell beside Judas.","Brutus_the_Younger","T","Assassination of Julius Caesar; Julius Caesar; Roman Republic; William Shakespeare"),
("Mir Jafar",1691,"1691–1765","Commander and Nawab of Bengal","Bengal","Betraying Siraj ud-Daulah at Plassey","Mir Jafar commanded the Bengal army under Nawab Siraj ud-Daulah. He secretly agreed with Robert Clive to stand aside at the Battle of Plassey in 1757 in exchange for becoming Nawab. After the battle his son had Siraj murdered. As Nawab he was a puppet, paying huge sums to the Company and its officers; when he could not meet demands, the British replaced him with his son-in-law Mir Qasim, then reinstated him. In India 'Mir Jafar' became a byword for traitor.","Mir_Jafar","T","Battle of Plassey; Battle of Plassey (battle); Robert Clive; East India Company rule"),
("Mir Sadiq",1750,"died 1799","Minister of Mysore","Mysore","Accused of betraying Tipu Sultan","Mir Sadiq was Tipu Sultan's chief minister and is widely remembered as having betrayed him during the British siege of Srirangapatna in 1799 — reportedly withdrawing soldiers from the breach and closing a gate behind Tipu. He was killed by Mysore soldiers during the fall of the city. Historians debate the details, but 'Mir Sadiq' became a symbol of betrayal in South India, alongside Mir Jafar.","Mir_Sadiq","T","Siege of Seringapatam (1799); Tipu Sultan; Kingdom of Mysore"),
("Benedict Arnold",1741,"1741–1801","American general turned traitor","USA","Plotting to surrender West Point to the British","Arnold was one of the American Revolution's best generals, wounded at Saratoga. Feeling unappreciated and in debt, he secretly negotiated with the British to hand over the fort at West Point in 1780. The plot was discovered when British officer John André was captured; Arnold escaped and fought for Britain. His name became a synonym for traitor in America.","Benedict_Arnold","T","American Revolution; George Washington"),
("Vidkun Quisling",1887,"1887–1945","Norwegian politician","Norway","Collaborating with Nazi Germany","Quisling led a small Norwegian fascist party and seized power with German backing when Germany invaded Norway in 1940, later heading a puppet government. He helped deport Norwegian Jews. After the war he was tried and executed by firing squad. 'Quisling' became an English word for a traitor who collaborates with an enemy occupier.","Vidkun_Quisling","T","World War II; Adolf Hitler; The Holocaust"),
("Khalid ibn al-Walid",585,"585–642","Arab general","Arabia, Syria","'The Sword of God'; undefeated commander","Khalid first fought against Muhammad, contributing to the Muslim defeat at Uhud, then converted around 629. He commanded in the Ridda wars and the conquest of Iraq and Syria, winning at Yarmouk (636) against the Byzantines, reportedly undefeated in over 100 battles. Caliph Umar dismissed him from supreme command, possibly fearing his popularity.","Khalid_ibn_al-Walid","R","Rashidun Caliphate; Battle of Qadisiyyah; Islam; Byzantine Empire"),
("Subutai",1175,"1175–1248","Mongol general","Mongolia","Strategist of Mongol conquests","Subutai, son of a blacksmith, rose to be Genghis Khan's greatest general, directing campaigns from China to Hungary and over 20 wars. He led the Mongol reconnaissance through the Caucasus to Russia (Battle of the Kalka, 1223) and planned the invasion of Europe, winning at Mohi in Hungary (1241).","Subutai","R","Mongol conquests; Genghis Khan; Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus'"),
("Bajirao I",1700,"1700–1740","Peshwa of the Maratha Empire","Maharashtra","Never lost a battle","Peshwa Bajirao I expanded Maratha power across central and northern India with lightning cavalry campaigns, defeating the Nizam at Palkhed (1728), a masterpiece of manoeuvre, and raiding Delhi in 1737. Field Marshal Montgomery praised Palkhed. His love for Mastani, a Muslim-Rajput princess, scandalised Pune's Brahmins.","Baji_Rao_I","R","Maratha Confederacy; Maratha Empire (Bhonsle and Peshwas)"),
("Hari Singh Nalwa",1791,"1791–1837","Sikh general","Punjab","Conquering Peshawar and holding the Khyber","Commander-in-chief of Ranjit Singh's Khalsa army, Hari Singh Nalwa captured Kashmir, Multan and Peshawar and built forts at the Khyber Pass. He died defending Jamrud fort against the Afghans in 1837.","Hari_Singh_Nalwa","R","Sikh Empire; Maharaja Ranjit Singh"),
("Rani Durgavati",1524,"1524–1564","Queen of the Gond kingdom of Garha","Madhya Pradesh","Fighting Akbar's army","A Chandela princess who married into the Gond royal family, Durgavati ruled the Gond kingdom of Garha-Katanga as regent, building reservoirs and defending it against Malwa. When Akbar's general Asaf Khan invaded in 1564, she fought on an elephant, was wounded by arrows and took her own life rather than be captured.","Rani_Durgavati","F","Gond; Akbar; Mughal Empire"),
("Rani Chennamma of Kittur",1778,"1778–1829","Queen","Karnataka","Early armed resistance to the East India Company","When the Company refused to recognise her adopted son under its doctrine of lapse-like policy, Chennamma fought the British in 1824, killing the collector St John Thackeray. She was captured in a second attack and died in prison.","Kittur_Chennamma","F","East India Company rule"),
("Tatya Tope",1814,"1814–1859","Rebel general","Central India","Leading the 1857 rebellion","Tatya Tope, a close aide of Nana Sahib, led rebel forces at Kanpur, joined Rani Lakshmibai at Gwalior and fought a long guerrilla campaign in central India. Betrayed by his friend Man Singh of Narwar, he was captured and hanged in 1859.","Tatya_Tope","F","Indian Rebellion of 1857; Rani Lakshmibai; Siege of Cawnpore and Bibighar"),
]
